## Code Snippet

1. Responsive Layout Fix (Mobile Optimization)

@media (max-width: 768px){
  .recipe-content{
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .ingredients,
  .directions{
    width: 100%;
  }
}

Explanation:
While building my recipe detail pages, I noticed that the ingredients and 
directions sections were overflowing on smaller screens. This was caused by using
a horizontal flex layout with fixed widths.
To solve this, I changed the layout to a vertical column on mobile devices and 
allowed both sections to take full width. This prevented horizontal scrolling and
improved readability.

2. CSS Variables for Design Consistency

:root{
  --c-primary:#DC143C;
  --c-bg:#ffffff;
  --c-text:#222;
  --c-muted:#666;
}

Explanation:
Instead of writing the same color values again and again in my CSS, I used CSS 
variables to store them in one place， which was taught by the professor.
For example, instead of repeatedly writing #DC143C, I defined it as --c-primary
and reused it across different elements like buttons, headers, and backgrounds.
This makes the code easier to manage, because if I want to change a color later, 
I only need to update it once in the :root, and it will automatically update 
everywhere. It also helps keep the design consistent across different pages.


3. Responsive Navigation Adjustment

@media (max-width: 768px){
  .category-nav {
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 6px;
  }

  .category-nav a {
    flex: 1;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 11px;
  }
}

Explanation:
I adjusted the navigation layout for mobile screens so that all category buttons 
stay on a single row. By using flex: 1, each button automatically takes equal 
width, and reducing the font size prevents overlap.
This improves usability on smaller devices and keeps the interface clean and
organized.


4. Hero and JavaScript

The layout is typically controlled using Flexbox in CSS, allowing the image and 
text to appear side by side.
In the JavaScript part, I used:

window.addEventListener("scroll", function () {

to detect when the user scrolls.

A trigger point is calculated using:

const triggerPoint = window.innerHeight * 0.7;

This means the animation will start when the user scrolls past 70% of the 
viewport height.

When this condition is met:

heroWrapper.classList.add("active");

an "active" class is added, which activates a CSS animation (such as fade-in 
and upward movement).
If the user scrolls back up, the class is removed, making the interaction 
reversible.

Explanation: 
This hero section uses a flex layout to combine an image and text, and a 
scroll-triggered animation is applied using JavaScript. When the user scrolls 
past 70% of the viewport height, an "active" class is added, triggering a smooth 
fade-in and upward motion defined in CSS.

